I'm starting to draft the review, checking the gear coupling math in profile_study.py — verifying that the rotation ratio between the shaper tool and workpiece teeth counts is applied correctly.

Though the magnitude of the ratio looks right, I realize the sign convention for the external gear pair's counter-rotation isn't actually verified in the code, so I flag that as unconfirmed. I'm also working through whether sampling one tool-pitch of alpha is sufficient to cover the full meshing cycle when the ratio N/10 is an integer, since each tool tooth might repeatedly meet the same work tooth sequence rather than sampling all combinations.

The real issue is whether the tool counts as a true conjugate shape: the correct derivation requires computing the tool's inner envelope as the intersection, across all relative poses, of the complement of the work solid — essentially the largest tool body that never penetrates the work. Expressing this as a polar radius function means trimming along each ray to the first contact point, which only works cleanly under a star-shaped assumption about the tool's geometry.

The inverse envelope gives the maximal non-interfering tool at the touching pose, but doesn't guarantee conjugacy — the forward check with residual stock is what actually verifies the work gets fully regenerated. I still need to check whether the tool-frame rays, cast from the tool origin at various angles, could cause interference issues at the root or tip.

Looking at how the composite rotation is applied, the tool centre orbits around the work origin as part of the rotation by γ=Nα/10, since the rotation is applied to the whole point including the centre offset rather than just the tool orientation — I need to double check this sign convention and whether rotating the centre along with the tool is actually correct for this gear-like coupling.

Working through the rotation mapping, I find θ_t = α and θ_w = -γ, meaning the tool orientation equals α while the work rotates counter to γ — since both use the same rotate function, the composite centre angle γ combines with α consistently in the formula.

This opposite-sign relationship actually makes sense for an external gear mesh, where the two bodies rotate in opposite directions in the lab frame, confirming the magnitude ratio N/10 is correct. Now I'm checking the pitch radius relationship: ω_w/ω_t = N/10 implies the work rotates faster than the tool when N exceeds 10, since the work has 10 teeth and the tool has N, and I need to verify this against r_t/r_w = ω_w/ω_t to make sure the centre distance C is consistently divided between the two pitch circles.

For a nonstandard profile, a conjugate cutter exists across a range of centre distances, but the tooth-flank envelope and resulting pitch point shift depending on which C is chosen, since the pitch point location is tied to where the work profile's normals actually intersect — so the centre distance isn't truly free once I'm demanding an exact conjugate relationship.

With a fixed ratio and C, the pitch point splits C accordingly, giving a work pitch radius of 10C/(10+N); checking that this falls between my root (4.29) and tip (6.08) radii confirms the chosen centres (giving r_p around 5.17–5.25) are valid. For the angular sampling and contact detection, I'm treating gap as a radial comparison in polar coordinates rather than true Euclidean distance, which only works cleanly if the work tooth profile is star-shaped (single-valued as a function of angle) — something I need to verify given the root/tip radii before trusting the ray-casting contact detection.

But this outward ray-cast from radius 20 only captures the outermost hit per direction, meaning it computes the star-hull envelope and silently ignores undercut or reentrant regions where the flank folds back — a real risk since 10 teeth with standard 20° pressure angle typically undergoes root undercutting (z=10 < 17). I should test for multi-valuedness by checking ray-hit counts rather than just the first hit, or better yet, extract the exact profile via a planar mesh section (like a bisect operation) instead of relying on 7200 angular samples at 0.05° resolution.


